    "https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-psychology-neuroscience-gonzaga-main-campus-washington-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Psychology (Neuroscience)",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Psychology (Neuroscience) - Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States Applicant Login My Profile Careers Home Diversity & Inclusion Benefits Clery Statement Internal Careers Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Assistant Professor, Psychology (Neuroscience) 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Closing at: Oct 26 2026 - 23:55 PDT Add to favorites View favorites Title: Assistant Professor, Psychology (Neuroscience) Area: College of Arts and Sciences Department: Psychology Telework Availability: On-campus Position Employee Classification: Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Appointment Percentage: 100 Months per year: 9 Salary Range: The budgeted annual salary that the University reasonably expects to offer for this position is $72,853. The internal salary range is $72,853 - $77,710. Is this position eligible for relocation reimbursement?: Yes Is this position eligible for a signing bonus?: No At this time, the University has suspended all new H1B visa sponsorships. Job Purpose: The Department of Psychology and Neuroscience at Gonzaga University in Spokane, WA invites applicants for a full-time Assistant Professor position (tenure-track) in Neuroscience beginning August 16, 2027. We are seeking a neuroscientist whose research investigates the neural mechanisms underlying human cognition, perception, and/or behavior. We welcome candidates from a range of areas within human neuroscience, with particular interest in researchers whose work could utilize our existing electroencephalography (EEG) and/or transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) facilities and provide meaningful research opportunities for undergraduate students. Gonzaga University is committed to pursuing excellence in teaching by creating engaging, inclusive learning environments for undergraduate students, and we are seeking a colleague who shares that commitment. Courses will include a mixture of introductory-level Psychology and Neuroscience courses and upper-division Neuroscience courses. We seek candidates who demonstrate an active program of research in neuroscience and who can mentor undergraduate students in their research. Ideal candidates will have a record of teaching undergraduates from a variety of backgrounds and identities, including those historically marginalized in the sciences (e.g., first-generation students, students from low-income families, BIPOC students, LGBTQ+ students, and students with disabilities). About Gonzaga University and Spokane, Washington: The Psychology and Neuroscience Department and the College of Arts and Sciences are committed to inclusive excellence, and the success of a diverse population of undergraduate students, staff, and faculty. Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission of educating students “for lives of leadership and service for the common good.” The University is committed to fostering “a mature commitment to dignity of the human person, social justice, diversity, intercultural competence, global engagement, solidarity with the poor and vulnerable, and care for the planet.” Successful applicants will have read the mission statement and engaged with it throughout their application. The University is a committed EEO/AA employer seeking to increase the diversity of its faculty, staff, and students. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Gonzaga’s campus sits adjacent to downtown Spokane, WA, along the Spokane River. We exist on the historical and current lands of Salish speaking tribes in the Inland Northwest’s most metropolitan region. Spokane is Washington’s second largest city and boasts a vibrant restaurant, entertainment, and arts community; it is also home to a diverse array of neighborhoods and popular outdoor recreational areas. For more information on our mission, please visit: https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement Essential Functions: Teach an equivalent to 9 credits each semester, typically three lecture sections (3 credits each) per semester. Teaching areas will be matched to candidates’ preparation and interests according to program and department needs, but will include a mixture of introductory-level Neuroscience courses, such as NEUR 201: Introduction to Neuroscience, and upper-division courses that support both the Neuroscience and Psychology majors. These may include BIOL 376: Cellular Neurophysiology, HPHY 451: Systems Neurophysiology, NEUR 399: Scientific Communication, NEUR 499: Neuroscience and Society, PSYC 300: Biological Psychology, PSYC 381: Affective Neuroscience, and PSYC 404: Psychology of Addiction, as well as advanced seminar courses in the candidate’s area of scholarly expertise. Develop a successful neuroscience research program providing quality research experiences to undergraduates. A successful researcher will publish in peer reviewed journals and seek external research funding. Provide academic and career advising and mentoring to 15 – 25 undergraduate students per year in the associated Neuroscience major, starting in the second year. Support the Psychology and Neuroscience department, the College of Arts and Sciences, and university through service and other forms of academic citizenship while maintaining close relationships with departments collaborating with the Neuroscience Program such as Biology and Human Physiology. Engage in professional activities that inform teaching, enhance student experience, and help the faculty member to remain current in their field. Maintain standards of professionalism. Minimum Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Neuroscience, Psychology, or a closely related field. At least one year experience teaching undergraduate students in relevant courses. Evidence of a research program focused on the neural mechanisms underlying human cognition, perception, and/or behavior that utilizes, or could be advanced through, methods such as electroencephalography (EEG) and/or transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS). Preferred Qualifications: At least one year of postdoctoral work or a similar experience. Evidence of engaging, inclusive teaching at the undergraduate level. Evidence of effectiveness in teaching and mentoring undergraduate students. Evidence of peer-reviewed scholarship. Educational Requirement: Terminal Degree Physical & On-Campus Requirements: In-Office Presence - Ability to work on-site in typical office setting and participate in in-person meetings with colleagues: 5 days/week Standing/Sitting - Ability to remain in a stationary position for a prolonged period: Often (50%-75% of time) Walking - Ability to move about on foot to accomplish tasks: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Lifting/Carrying - Ability to move or transport objects up to a specified weight: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Attention to Detail - Ability to focus on detailed tasks for extended periods: Daily (>75% of time) Problem-Solving - Ability to identify and resolve issues effectively: Daily (>75% of time) Required Application Materials: • A Cover Letter which summarizes the areas above and describes the candidates understanding of and commitment to the university’s mission. • Curriculum vitae. • A statement of teaching philosophy (2-page max) referencing evidence of teaching effectiveness and describing how the candidate creates and maintains an inclusive environment that can support and promote learning for students with a variety of backgrounds and identities. • Evidence of effective teaching and or sample materials from relevant courses, if available. • A research statement (2-pages max) that describes the candidate’s program of research and how they will mentor undergraduate students in their research program at Gonzaga • Copies of unofficial graduate school transcripts. Names and contact information for three professional references will be collected from advancing candidates during the interview process. References will be sent a link to upload a confidential letter of recommendation. The position will close October 26, 2026. Questions about this position can be directed to Dr. Kevin Measor, program director, at measor@gonzaga.edu. For assistance with your online application, please contact Human Resources at (509) 313-5996. Total Rewards Statement At Gonzaga, we value the well-being and contributions of our colleagues by offering a comprehensive total rewards package. In compliance with Washington State's Pay Transparency Act, we provide both the full salary range for each position, and an anticipated salary range that reflects the budgeted amount that the university reasonable expects to offer the position. In addition to base compensation, we offer a competitive benefits package, which may vary depending on the position type and hours worked. To explore the full range of benefits available, please review the applicable Prospective Employee Benefit Guide available on the Gonzaga website. EEO Statement Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission. Gonzaga University is a committed EEO/AA employer and diversity candidates are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Applicants with disabilities needing reasonable accommodations to complete the application or hiring process should contact Human Resources (509) 313-5996. Recommended jobs Assistant Director for Native Student Recruitment and Initiatives 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tribal Relations Help shape a welcoming and empowering experience for Native students while building meaningful partnerships with tribal communities across the region. Join Gonzaga as the Assistant Director of Native Student Programs and lead innovative recruitment, engagement, and student success initiatives that make a lasting impact. Admissions Specialist 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Graduate Enrollment Management Ready for a role that combines travel, relationship-building, and meaningful impact? Join Gonzaga University's Graduate Admissions team and connect prospective students with a graduate education that helps them achieve their personal and professional goals. Admission Application Readers 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Admissions Love reading inspiring stories and spotting future leaders? Help shape the next generation of Gonzaga Bulldogs by reviewing college applications, discovering students' unique achievements and potential, and collaborating with a dedicated admissions team to build a vibrant, talented, and mission-driven campus community. Assessment and Accreditation Officer, SOE 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will provide support and leadership to all departments in the School of Education in assessment and evaluation of programs and strategic initiatives. The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will sustain overall assessment, accreditation, and reporting for the School of Education. Adjunct Instructor, Teacher Certification Supervisor 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education Gonzaga University’s School of Education Teacher Certification Program is currently seeking certified teachers interested in supervising Pre-Service Teacher Candidates at the K-12 levels, in all content areas in the Greater Spokane Area for the academic year.\n\nA Teacher Certification Supervisor is a mentor and a liaison in coordinating the functions between the teacher candidates, mentor teachers, and the department. Adjunct Instructor, Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Leadership Studies The Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies at Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington invites applications for its Adjunct Instructor Pool. This pool focuses on teaching doctoral-level research courses, including classes such as Principles of Research and Quantitative Research Methods. Return to Job Search Diversity & Inclusion At Gonzaga University, we are committed to fostering an inclusive and diverse community where differences in age, gender, ethnicity, disability, social class, religion, culture, sexual orientation, and language enrich our campus. We value mutual respect and aim to create a welcoming, accessible environment for all members of our community, in line with our Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission. Gonzaga University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages diversity candidates to apply. All qualified applicants will be considered without regard to disability, veteran status, gender, race, or other protected categories. Categories Staff Careers Faculty Careers Quick Links Spokane & Community Our Mission Privacy Policy GU Home Copyright © All Rights Reserved by Gonzaga University . Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
