    "https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/lecturer-political-science-gonzaga-main-campus-washington-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer, Political Science",
      "description": "Lecturer, Political Science - Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States Applicant Login My Profile Careers Home Diversity & Inclusion Benefits Clery Statement Internal Careers Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Lecturer, Political Science 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Closing at: Sep 28 2026 - 23:55 PDT Add to favorites View favorites Title: Lecturer, Political Science Area: College of Arts and Sciences Department: Political Science Telework Availability: On-campus Position Employee Classification: Non-Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Appointment Percentage: 100 Months per year: 9 Salary Range: The budgeted annual salary that the University reasonably expects to offer for this position is $75,364. The internal salary range is $75,364 - $80,388. Is this position eligible for relocation reimbursement?: Yes Is this position eligible for a signing bonus?: No At this time, the University has suspended all new H1B visa sponsorships. Job Purpose: The Department of Political Science at Gonzaga University in Spokane, WA invites applications for a full-time, nine-month, non-tenure-track Lecturer position in Comparative Politics, with a specialization in Latin America, beginning August 16, 2027. This position is renewable annually, subject to satisfactory performance review and continuing curricular and institutional need. Gonzaga University is committed to pursuing excellence in teaching by creating engaging, inclusive learning environments for undergraduate students, and we are seeking a colleague who shares that commitment. The successful candidate will teach a mixture of introductory-level Comparative Politics courses, upper-division courses on Latin American politics, and additional courses informed by the candidate's areas of expertise and the department's curricular needs. The standard teaching load is 4/4 (generally five unique course preparations per year), entirely at the undergraduate level. We seek candidates who bring an active commitment to undergraduate teaching and mentorship, and who are drawn to Gonzaga's Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission of educating the whole person. About Gonzaga University and Spokane, Washington Founded in 1887, Gonzaga is a private, liberal arts institution located in Spokane, WA. Gonzaga’s mission reflects its Catholic, Jesuit, and humanistic identity. The Department of Political Science awards the BA degree and enjoys a growing enrollment of politically involved social justice-minded students. Our average class size is under 25, enabling our faculty to invest in engaged pedagogy and transformational teaching. Gonzaga has been ranked in the top 20 for excellence in undergraduate teaching among National Universities by US News and World Report. Gonzaga’s 152-acre campus sits along the Spokane River within walking distance of downtown Spokane, on the ancestral homelands of the Spokane Tribe of Indians. Spokane is the second largest city in Washington and pairs urban advantages, including an international airport, a vibrant arts community, and a range of exciting restaurants, with easy access to mountains, rivers, and lakes. Located on the east side of Washington State, Spokane offers many options for outdoor enjoyment including hiking, boating and fishing, and several ski resorts within driving distance. For more information on our mission, please visit: https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement Essential Functions: Teach the equivalent of 24 credits per academic year. The successful candidate will typically teach two sections of the department’s introductory course in comparative politics each semester and regularly offer our upper division survey of Latin American Politics and another course grounded in this region. The remaining teaching portfolio will be rounded out by additional courses required for the political science major and minor, along with courses aligned with the successful candidate's areas of expertise. Support the department, university, community and discipline through service and other forms of academic citizenship. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Political Science or international equivalent (ABD will be considered if the degree will be conferred by July 31, 2027) Demonstrated specialization in Comparative Politics with a regional focus on Latin America Evidence of effectiveness in teaching undergraduate students from a variety of backgrounds, identities, and experiences, including those historically marginalized in higher education. Prior experience as an instructor of record is preferred but not required. Demonstrated understanding of and commitment to Gonzaga's mission, including its emphasis on cura personalis (care for the whole person) and educating students for lives of leadership, justice, and service. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated expertise, ability, and willingness to teach in one or more of the following: comparative populist or other social movements; comparative electoral systems or party politics; decoloniality; migration and border politics; indigenous politics; comparative political economy; or other issues centering the experiences of the Global South. Ability and willingness to teach an introductory course on political inquiry and analysis as coverage needs arise. Ability and willingness to teach occasional courses in other subfields (e.g., International Relations, American Politics, Political Theory) as departmental needs require. Experience or interest in contributing to an interdisciplinary International Studies program. Experience advising and mentoring undergraduate students, and a demonstrated ability to work collaboratively within a teaching-oriented department. Educational Requirement: Terminal Degree Physical & On-Campus Requirements: In-Office Presence - Ability to work on-site in typical office setting and participate in in-person meetings with colleagues: 5 days/week Standing/Sitting - Ability to remain in a stationary position for a prolonged period: Often (50%-75% of time) Walking - Ability to move about on foot to accomplish tasks: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Lifting/Carrying - Ability to move or transport objects up to a specified weight: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Attention to Detail - Ability to focus on detailed tasks for extended periods: Daily (>75% of time) Problem-Solving - Ability to identify and resolve issues effectively: Daily (>75% of time) Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit our website at www.gonzaga.edu/jobs. Applicants must complete an online application and electronically submit: (1) a cover letter addressing the applicant's qualifications for teaching in comparative politics with a specialization in Latin America, including the specific courses the applicant is prepared to teach, and describing the candidate's understanding of and commitment to the university's mission, (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) a one- to two-page statement of teaching effectiveness that includes evidence such as formal or informal teaching evaluations, (4) unofficial copies of graduate school transcripts. The names and email addresses of three persons who will be writing letters of recommendation will be collected from advancing candidates during the interview process (letters will be solicited when finalists are selected). This position closes on September 28, 2026. For assistance with your online application, please contact Human Resources at (509) 313.5996. Direct other inquiries to the chair of Political Science, Dr. Cynthia Stavrianos, stavrianos@gonzaga.edu. Total Rewards Statement At Gonzaga, we value the well-being and contributions of our colleagues by offering a comprehensive total rewards package. In compliance with Washington State's Pay Transparency Act, we provide both the full salary range for each position, and an anticipated salary range that reflects the budgeted amount that the university reasonable expects to offer the position. In addition to base compensation, we offer a competitive benefits package, which may vary depending on the position type and hours worked. To explore the full range of benefits available, please review the applicable Prospective Employee Benefit Guide available on the Gonzaga website. EEO Statement Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission. Gonzaga University is a committed EEO/AA employer and diversity candidates are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Applicants with disabilities needing reasonable accommodations to complete the application or hiring process should contact Human Resources (509) 313-5996. Recommended jobs Assistant Director for Native Student Recruitment and Initiatives 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tribal Relations Help shape a welcoming and empowering experience for Native students while building meaningful partnerships with tribal communities across the region. Join Gonzaga as the Assistant Director of Native Student Programs and lead innovative recruitment, engagement, and student success initiatives that make a lasting impact. Admissions Specialist 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Graduate Enrollment Management Ready for a role that combines travel, relationship-building, and meaningful impact? Join Gonzaga University's Graduate Admissions team and connect prospective students with a graduate education that helps them achieve their personal and professional goals. Admission Application Readers 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Admissions Love reading inspiring stories and spotting future leaders? Help shape the next generation of Gonzaga Bulldogs by reviewing college applications, discovering students' unique achievements and potential, and collaborating with a dedicated admissions team to build a vibrant, talented, and mission-driven campus community. Assessment and Accreditation Officer, SOE 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will provide support and leadership to all departments in the School of Education in assessment and evaluation of programs and strategic initiatives. The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will sustain overall assessment, accreditation, and reporting for the School of Education. Adjunct Instructor, Teacher Certification Supervisor 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education Gonzaga University’s School of Education Teacher Certification Program is currently seeking certified teachers interested in supervising Pre-Service Teacher Candidates at the K-12 levels, in all content areas in the Greater Spokane Area for the academic year.\n\nA Teacher Certification Supervisor is a mentor and a liaison in coordinating the functions between the teacher candidates, mentor teachers, and the department. Adjunct Instructor, Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Leadership Studies The Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies at Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington invites applications for its Adjunct Instructor Pool. This pool focuses on teaching doctoral-level research courses, including classes such as Principles of Research and Quantitative Research Methods. Return to Job Search Diversity & Inclusion At Gonzaga University, we are committed to fostering an inclusive and diverse community where differences in age, gender, ethnicity, disability, social class, religion, culture, sexu