    "https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-law-gonzaga-main-campus-washington-united-states-54f9e6b6-4c4b-4a46-a97f-601403067b88": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Law",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Law - Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States Applicant Login My Profile Careers Home Diversity & Inclusion Benefits Clery Statement Internal Careers Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Assistant Professor, Law 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Closing at: Oct 16 2026 - 23:55 PDT Add to favorites View favorites Title: Assistant Professor, Law Area: School of Law Department: Law Academic Year on Campus Telework Availability: On-campus Position Employee Classification: Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Appointment Percentage: 100 Months per year: 9 Salary Range: The budgeted annual salary that the University reasonably expects to offer for this position is $106,084. The internal salary range is $106,084 - $113,156 (commensurate with education and experience). Is this position eligible for relocation reimbursement?: Yes At this time, the University has suspended all new H1B visa sponsorships. Job Purpose: Full-time professors, regardless of rank, will typically teach two courses per semester, which may include first-year and/or required courses. Our curricular needs include a variety of first-year, required, and elective courses, including Evidence, Criminal Law, Property, Business Associations, Sales, Wills and Trusts, Environmental Law, Intellectual Property Law, Legal Research and Writing, and skills-related courses. Candidates for Assistant Professor must demonstrate strong potential for excellence in teaching, a commitment to service, and excellent scholarly potential, particularly in areas aligned with Gonzaga Law’s two academic Centers – the Center for Civil & Human Rights and the Kennelly Center for Law, Ethics & Commerce. Assistant professors are supported as scholars across all subject areas and have the opportunity to teach doctrinal, experiential, clinical, academic support, or bar preparation courses if desired. Gonzaga Law is dedicated to its humanistic mission of educating the whole person and preparing lawyers to serve marginalized communities in an increasingly global landscape. Faculty members teach and mentor students, provide service to the law school and University, and engage with the broader profession and public to advance the exchange of ideas, improve the law, and promote the common good. For more information on our mission, please visit: https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement Essential Functions: Instructional Duties: • Teach law school courses in areas of expertise, including Evidence, Criminal Law, Property, Business Associations, Sales, Wills and Trusts, Environmental Law, Intellectual Property Law, Legal Research and Writing, and skills-related courses. • Plan, evaluate, and revise curricula, course content, course materials, and methods of instruction for law students. • Supervise and advise students enrolled in self-directed legal research and writing projects. •Engage with student advisees to plan their law school curriculum. Professional Development: ● Contribute to the intellectual exchange of ideas, enrich teaching, and educate the profession about the law. ●Engage in continuing professional development as a member of the academic community, including participation in Gonzaga Law’s two academic centers. ● Significant publication is required. Professional development as a scholar may also include: o Presentations to academic, professional, and community groups; o Participation in activities of regional, national, or international professional organizations; o Participation in workshops or seminars; and o Selection for grants, fellowships, and awards. Administrative Duties: • Devote considerable time to non-teaching activities essential to the operation of a law school committed to excellence. Responsibilities may include: o Attending meetings of the law faculty; o Being actively involved in law school and University committees; o Advising law students; o Maintaining regular office hours; o Performing service to the University and to the public; o Complying with ABA and University policies; and o Cooperating with the school’s efforts to comply with applicable law. Minimum Qualifications: ● Juris Doctor or equivalent degree earned from an ABA-accredited law school or its equivalent earned by July 31st, 2027. ● Demonstrated record of academic achievement. ● Strong professional recommendations. ● Demonstrated excellence in teaching or clear potential to become an excellent teacher. ● Evidence of scholarly research agenda and potential for scholarly excellence Preferred Qualifications: ● Six months of law school or clinical teaching experience. ● Twelve months of legal practice experience in one or more areas aligned with our curricular needs. ● Demonstrated teaching effectiveness, including the ability to support learners from underrepresented and diverse backgrounds. ● Strong interpersonal communication, leadership, collaboration, innovation, and organizational skills. ● Commitment to collegiality, self-awareness, and a healthy sense of humor. ● Active law licensure in a state bar of the United States, and/or the ability and interest to become actively licensed to practice law in Washington state. ● At least one law review publication or its equivalent. Educational Requirement: Terminal Degree Physical & On-Campus Requirements: In-Office Presence - Ability to work on-site in typical office setting and participate in in-person meetings with colleagues: 5 days/week Standing/Sitting - Ability to remain in a stationary position for a prolonged period: Daily (>75% of time) Walking - Ability to move about on foot to accomplish tasks: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Lifting/Carrying - Ability to move or transport objects up to a specified weight: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Attention to Detail - Ability to focus on detailed tasks for extended periods: Daily (>75% of time) Problem-Solving - Ability to identify and resolve issues effectively: Daily (>75% of time) Required Application Materials: (1) a cover letter, (2) a curriculum vitae, (3) a teaching statement addressing teaching philosophy, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and experience fostering an inclusive learning environment, (4) a research agenda, (5) law school teaching evaluations (if available), and (6) names and contact information for three professional references will be requested from advancing candidates during the interview process. Questions may be directed to Michael Cecil, Chair of the Faculty Recruitment Committee, at lawfacultyhiring@gonzaga.edu. Applicants must also apply through Gonzaga University’s Office of Human Resources at the link above. For assistance with the online application, contact Human Resources at 509-313-5996. The position closes October 16, 2026, at 11:55pm (U.S. PST). Total Rewards Statement At Gonzaga, we value the well-being and contributions of our colleagues by offering a comprehensive total rewards package. In compliance with Washington State's Pay Transparency Act, we provide both the full salary range for each position, and an anticipated salary range that reflects the budgeted amount that the university reasonable expects to offer the position. In addition to base compensation, we offer a competitive benefits package, which may vary depending on the position type and hours worked. To explore the full range of benefits available, please review the applicable Prospective Employee Benefit Guide available on the Gonzaga website. EEO Statement Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission. Gonzaga University is a committed EEO/AA employer and diversity candidates are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Applicants with disabilities needing reasonable accommodations to complete the application or hiring process should contact Human Resources (509) 313-5996. Content missing Recommended jobs Assistant Director for Native Student Recruitment and Initiatives 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tribal Relations Help shape a welcoming and empowering experience for Native students while building meaningful partnerships with tribal communities across the region. Join Gonzaga as the Assistant Director of Native Student Programs and lead innovative recruitment, engagement, and student success initiatives that make a lasting impact. Admissions Specialist 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Graduate Enrollment Management Ready for a role that combines travel, relationship-building, and meaningful impact? Join Gonzaga University's Graduate Admissions team and connect prospective students with a graduate education that helps them achieve their personal and professional goals. Admission Application Readers 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Admissions Love reading inspiring stories and spotting future leaders? Help shape the next generation of Gonzaga Bulldogs by reviewing college applications, discovering students' unique achievements and potential, and collaborating with a dedicated admissions team to build a vibrant, talented, and mission-driven campus community. Assessment and Accreditation Officer, SOE 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will provide support and leadership to all departments in the School of Education in assessment and evaluation of programs and strategic initiatives. The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will sustain overall assessment, accreditation, and reporting for the School of Education. Adjunct Instructor, Teacher Certification Supervisor 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education Gonzaga University’s School of Education Teacher Certification Program is currently seeking certified teachers interested in supervising Pre-Service Teacher Candidates at the K-12 levels, in all content areas in the Greater Spokane Area for the academic year.\n\nA Teacher Certification Supervisor is a mentor and a liaison in coordinating the functions between the teacher candidates, mentor teachers, and the department. Adjunct Instructor, Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Leadership Studies The Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies at Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington invites applications for its Adjunct Instructor Pool. This pool focuses on teaching doctoral-level research courses, including classes such as Principles of Research and Quantitative Research Methods. Return to Job Search Content missing Diversity & Inclusion At Gonzaga University, we are committed to fostering an inclusive and diverse community where differences in age, gender, ethnicity, disability, social class, religion, culture, sexual orientation, and language enrich our campus. We value mutual respect and aim to create a welcoming, accessible environment for all members of our community, in line with our Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission. Gonzaga University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages diversity candidates to apply. All qualified applicants will be considered without regard to disability, veteran status, gender, race, or other protected categories. Categories Staff Careers Faculty Careers Quick Links Spokane & Community Our Mission Privacy Policy GU Home Copyright © 2026 All Rights Reserved by Gonzaga University . Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content