    "https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-electrical-and-computer-engineering-gonzaga-main-campus-washington-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Electrical and Computer Engineering - Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States Applicant Login My Profile Careers Home Diversity & Inclusion Benefits Clery Statement Internal Careers Applicant Login My Profile Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Assistant Professor, Electrical and Computer Engineering 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Closing at: Oct 1 2026 - 23:55 PDT Add to favorites View favorites Title: Assistant Professor, Electrical and Computer Engineering Area: School of Engineering & Applied Science Department: Electrical Engineering Telework Availability: On-campus Position Employee Classification: Tenure Track Faculty (Full-time) Appointment Percentage: 100 Months per year: 9 Salary Range: The budgeted annual salary that the University reasonably expects to offer for this position is $91,495. The internal salary range is $91,495 - $97,595. Is this position eligible for relocation reimbursement?: Yes Is this position eligible for a signing bonus?: No At this time, the University has suspended all new H1B visa sponsorships. Job Purpose: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Gonzaga University in Spokane, WA invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position beginning Fall 2027. The department seeks a teacher-scholar committed to inclusive, equity-minded undergraduate teaching and an active program of scholarship in electrical and/or computer engineering. This appointment is intended to strengthen the department’s curricular and scholarly capacity in digital hardware, computer engineering, and integrated electronic systems while supporting Gonzaga’s teaching-centered and mission-driven environment. The department is especially interested in candidates whose expertise can support courses and laboratories in areas such as digital system design, computer hardware design and architecture, embedded systems, VLSI or integrated circuits, and related electrical/computer engineering topics. Candidates with complementary expertise in electronic circuits, semiconductor devices, FPGA/HDL-based design, or closely related areas are also encouraged to apply. Applicants are requested to describe in their cover letter which existing ECE courses they are prepared to teach immediately and what new technical elective(s), laboratory experiences, or senior design projects they would be prepared to develop. The successful candidate will teach the equivalent of 18 semester credit hours per academic year, typically three 3-credit courses per semester. The department is committed to excellence in undergraduate teaching, attentive advising, extensive laboratory experiences, and sustained faculty engagement with professional development and scholarly activity. The Electrical and Computer Engineering Department offers ABET-accredited undergraduate programs in Electrical Engineering and Computer Engineering. Gonzaga seeks candidates who will contribute to a welcoming campus climate and to its Catholic, Jesuit, and humanistic mission. In keeping with the recruiting workflow, application materials for this position are designed to address scholarly achievement, pedagogy, and the candidate’s ability to contribute to Gonzaga’s commitment to human dignity, social justice, diversity, intercultural competence, global engagement, solidarity with the poor and vulnerable, and care for the planet. With a mission rooted in the Jesuit tradition, Gonzaga seeks to educate students to become compassionate leaders who engage with the world, seek justice, and contribute to the common good. The university mission statement is available at https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement. As a campus community, Gonzaga prioritizes inclusive excellence and increasing campus diversity, particularly in supporting students from backgrounds historically underrepresented in higher education. The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering seeks candidates who align with this mission and who can articulate, through teaching, mentoring, scholarship, and service, how they would advance it. Gonzaga’s campus sits adjacent to downtown Spokane, WA, along the Spokane River in the Inland Northwest’s most metropolitan region. The university exists on the traditional homelands of the Spokane people, one of the Interior Salish speaking tribes of the region. Spokane is Washington’s second-largest city and offers a vibrant arts, food, and outdoor community. For more information on our mission, please visit: https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement Essential Functions: Teach 18 credit hours of courses per year. Initial teaching responsibilities are expected to include one or more courses in areas such as digital system design, computer hardware design and architecture, embedded systems, electronics, VLSI/integrated circuits, or other courses aligned with departmental needs. There may also be opportunities to teach first-year engineering courses, senior technical electives in the candidate’s area of expertise, and courses within the senior design sequence. - (Essential) Establish and sustain a scholarly program in the candidate’s area of expertise. Candidates are encouraged to develop scholarship that supports undergraduate student participation, to mentor undergraduate researchers, and to disseminate outcomes through publications, presentations, creative work, prototypes, or other discipline-appropriate forms. - (Essential) Provide academic and career advising and mentoring to undergraduate students. - (Essential) Support the Department, University, and profession through service and other forms of academic citizenship. - (Essential) Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related field by July 15, 2027. Evidence of teaching effectiveness, mentorship, or strong promise of excellence in undergraduate teaching. Demonstrated expertise in one or more of the following areas: digital systems, computer hardware/architecture, embedded systems, VLSI or integrated circuits, or closely related ECE fields aligned with departmental needs. Evidence of an ongoing or clearly developing scholarly/research agenda. Ability to contribute to Gonzaga’s Catholic, Jesuit, humanistic mission and to create welcoming learning environments for students with a variety of backgrounds and identities. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of engaging, inclusive teaching practices in undergraduate engineering education. Record of course and laboratory development in electrical and/or computer engineering. Ability to teach and develop laboratory-rich courses in digital design, computer architecture, embedded systems, electronics, VLSI, or related areas. Demonstrated ability to establish a research program that meaningfully involves undergraduate students. Evidence of relevant postdoctoral, industrial, other professional work record in a field aligned with the position. Excellent communication skills in spoken and written English and a demonstrated interest in advising undergraduate students. Record of working with students from backgrounds historically underrepresented in engineering through teaching, research, mentoring, outreach, or administration. Educational Requirement: Terminal Degree Physical & On-Campus Requirements: In-Office Presence - Ability to work on-site in typical office setting and participate in in-person meetings with colleagues: 5 days/week Standing/Sitting - Ability to remain in a stationary position for a prolonged period: Daily (>75% of time) Walking - Ability to move about on foot to accomplish tasks: Daily (>75% of time) Lifting/Carrying - Ability to move or transport objects up to a specified weight: Often (50%-75% of time) Attention to Detail - Ability to focus on detailed tasks for extended periods: Daily (>75% of time) Problem-Solving - Ability to identify and resolve issues effectively: Daily (>75% of time) Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit Gonzaga’s employment website at https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/search. Applicants must complete an online application and submit the following materials: 1. A curriculum vitae. 2. A statement of teaching philosophy that includes: evidence of teaching effectiveness or teaching promise, a description of how the candidate creates and maintains an environment that supports learning for students with a variety of backgrounds and identities, and a description of how the candidate would advance Gonzaga’s mission and commitment to inclusive excellence through teaching and advising. 3. A statement describing the candidate’s research agenda and explaining how it can support undergraduate research experiences. The statement should also describe, where applicable, the candidate’s experience mentoring undergraduates and how their scholarship can contribute to Gonzaga’s mission. 4. A cover letter summarizing the candidate’s preparation for the position, addressing the candidate’s understanding of and commitment to Gonzaga’s Mission, and identifying the existing courses, new technical elective(s), and/or senior project areas the candidate would be prepared to offer or supervise. Names and contact information for three professional references will be collected from advancing candidates during the interview process. References will be sent a link to upload a confidential letter of recommendation. Please direct questions about this position to Vladimir Labay, Search Chair, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, labay@gonzaga.edu. For consideration, please submit materials by October 1, 2026, midnight, U.S. Pacific Time. For assistance with the online application, please call the Human Resources Department at 509-313-5996. Total Rewards Statement At Gonzaga, we value the well-being and contributions of our colleagues by offering a comprehensive total rewards package. In compliance with Washington State's Pay Transparency Act, we provide both the full salary range for each position, and an anticipated salary range that reflects the budgeted amount that the university reasonable expects to offer the position. In addition to base compensation, we offer a competitive benefits package, which may vary depending on the position type and hours worked. To explore the full range of benefits available, please review the applicable Prospective Employee Benefit Guide available on the Gonzaga website. EEO Statement Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission. Gonzaga University is a committed EEO/AA employer and diversity candidates are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Applicants with disabilities needing reasonable accommodations to complete the application or hiring process should contact Human Resources (509) 313-5996. Apply now First Name (required) 2df32b7d Last Name (required) b92d3364 Email (required) 4ed07fc5 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended jobs Assistant Director for Native Student Recruitment and Initiatives 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Tribal Relations Help shape a welcoming and empowering experience for Native students while building meaningful partnerships with tribal communities across the region. Join Gonzaga as the Assistant Director of Native Student Programs and lead innovative recruitment, engagement, and student success initiatives that make a lasting impact. Admissions Specialist 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Graduate Enrollment Management Ready for a role that combines travel, relationship-building, and meaningful impact? Join Gonzaga University's Graduate Admissions team and connect prospective students with a graduate education that helps them achieve their personal and professional goals. Admission Application Readers 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 Admissions Love reading inspiring stories and spotting future leaders? Help shape the next generation of Gonzaga Bulldogs by reviewing college applications, discovering students' unique achievements and potential, and collaborating with a dedicated admissions team to build a vibrant, talented, and mission-driven campus community. Assessment and Accreditation Officer, SOE 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will provide support and leadership to all departments in the School of Education in assessment and evaluation of programs and strategic initiatives. The Assessment and Accreditation Officer will sustain overall assessment, accreditation, and reporting for the School of Education. Adjunct Instructor, Teacher Certification Supervisor 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Education Gonzaga University’s School of Education Teacher Certification Program is currently seeking certified teachers interested in supervising Pre-Service Teacher Candidates at the K-12 levels, in all content areas in the Greater Spokane Area for the academic year.\n\nA Teacher Certification Supervisor is a mentor and a liaison in coordinating the functions between the teacher candidates, mentor teachers, and the department. Adjunct Instructor, Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies 502 East Boone Avenue, Logan, Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States, 99258 School of Leadership Studies The Doctoral Program in Leadership Studies at Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington invites applications for its Adjunct Instructor Pool. This pool focuses on teaching doctoral-level research courses, including classes such as Principles of Research and Quantitative Research Methods. Return to Job Search Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 59d2efd0 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments School of Engineering & App Science School of Engineering & App Science Locations Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States Gonzaga Main Campus, Washington, United States First Name (required) 07413420 Last Name (required) b400f0a0 Email (required) c6a61f93 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Diversity & Inclusion At Gonzaga University, we are committed to fostering an inclusive and diverse community where differences in age, gender, ethnicity, disability, social class, religion, culture, sexual orientation, and language enrich our campus. We value mutual respect and aim to create a welcoming, accessible environment for all members of our community, in line with our Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission. Gonzaga University is an equal opportunity employer and encourages diversity candidates to apply. All qualified applicants will be considered without regard to disability, veteran status, gender, race, or other protected categories. Categories Staff Careers Faculty Careers Quick Links Spokane & Community Our Mission Privacy Policy GU Home Copyright © 2026 All Rights Reserved by Gonzaga University . Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
